<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Knights Hospitaller</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1113–</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Order of Saint John, Knights of Malta, Knights of Rhodes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Knights Hospitaller, the Order of Saint John, grew from a hospital for pilgrims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> into a military order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a>. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> they ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> from 1310 to 1522 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> from 1530 to 1798, fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at sea. They also held Tripoli in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, estates across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and, briefly, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> islands bought in 1651.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller.jpg" alt="Knights Hospitaller" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Constantine Plakidas · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_Knights_Hospitaller.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>To hold the frontier, kings relied on the military orders, especially the Templars and <b>Hospitallers</b>, and on town militias raised under the fueros, and Alfonso II founded Teruel around 1170 to guard the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 1120s and 1130s the military orders, the Knights Templar and the <b>Knights Hospitaller</b>, and later the Teutonic Knights, provided permanent garrisons and the money to maintain them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The early kingdom was made by war against al-Andalus, fought by royal hosts, town militias and the military orders of the Templars, <b>Hospitallers</b>, Santiago and Avis, and later the Order of Christ, which Denis founded in 1319 to take over the Templars’ property.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1530 the <b>Knights Hospitaller</b> held Malta as a fief of the kingdom, paying the king of Sicily an annual tribute of a single falcon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>A Latin hierarchy headed by the Archbishop of Patras replaced the Orthodox bishops, and Cistercian, Franciscan and Dominican houses, along with estates of the Templars, <b>Hospitallers</b> and Teutonic Knights, were founded, but Orthodox priests kept their parishes and most of the population remained Orthodox.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Grand Master, elected for life by the brethren, governed with a small council of great officers: the Grand Commander, the Marshal, the <b>Hospitaller</b>, the Drapier and the Treasurer.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
